From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Siyuan Shen <siyshen@nvidia.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 08:46:59 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 5/9] Decouple NCCL_ALGO from NCCL_SYM_KERNEL when clearing
 tuning tables

Setting either NCCL_ALGO or NCCL_SYM_KERNEL cleared both the algorithm table
and the symmetric-kernel table. Forcing a symmetric kernel for one collective
therefore also wiped ordinary algorithm selection for every other collective.

Each variable now clears only its own table.

Signed-off-by: Siyuan Shen <siyshen@nvidia.com>
---
 src/tuning/cost_model.cc | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/tuning/cost_model.cc b/src/tuning/cost_model.cc
index f50e28ef8..b01801c83 100644
--- a/src/tuning/cost_model.cc
+++ b/src/tuning/cost_model.cc
@@ -324,8 +324,14 @@ ncclResult_t ncclTuningCostModelInit(struct ncclComm* comm) {
   const char* protoStr = ncclGetEnv("NCCL_PROTO");
   const char* algoStr = ncclGetEnv("NCCL_ALGO");
   const char* symKernelIdStr = ncclGetEnv("NCCL_SYM_KERNEL");
-  if ((algoStr && strlen(algoStr) > 0) || (symKernelIdStr && strlen(symKernelIdStr) > 0)) {
+  // Clear only the table the user actually constrained. Clearing both whenever
+  // either variable is set makes it impossible to force a symmetric kernel
+  // while leaving ordinary algorithm selection intact, which leaves collectives
+  // outside the allowlist with no valid route at all.
+  if (algoStr && strlen(algoStr) > 0) {
     std::fill_n(algoEnable, NCCL_NUM_FUNCTIONS * NCCL_NUM_ALGORITHMS, 0);
+  }
+  if (symKernelIdStr && strlen(symKernelIdStr) > 0) {
     std::fill_n(symKernelIdEnable, NCCL_NUM_FUNCTIONS * ncclSymkKernelId_Count, 0);
   }
   if (protoStr) {
